### Rubric levels

Every checkpoint is scored on a published **100-point analytic rubric**. Each rubric lists weighted criteria, and each criterion is scored at one of four levels:

| Level | Points | What it means |
|---|---|---|
| **Exemplary** | 4 | Professional quality. Could serve as a model for the class. |
| **Proficient** | 3 | Solid and complete; meets the founder-grade bar. |
| **Developing** | 2 | Present but thin, uneven, or partly missing. Needs another pass. |
| **Beginning** | 1 | Missing, superficial, or seriously flawed. |
| *(Absent)* | 0 | Not submitted; no evidence. |

A criterion contributes **(level ÷ 4) × its weight**, and the criteria weights sum to 100 within each assignment. That 100-point score then counts toward the course weight in the table above.

**Do the arithmetic on that, because it is not what most students assume.** Proficient is 3 of 4, which is **75% — a C.** Solid, complete work that meets the bar, scored Proficient across every criterion of every checkpoint, earns you a C in this course. Proficient is the floor for competent work, not the target.

**Aim for Exemplary everywhere.** That is the standard the rubrics are written against, and it is reachable: the criteria say plainly what Exemplary looks like, and the major artifacts can be revised after feedback. Most students work this out for themselves somewhere around the second checkpoint — you now know it before the first.

Rubrics are published with the assignment, not after it. Read the rubric before you start writing — knowing what a criterion rewards is most of the work of satisfying it.

### Pass conditions

Passing this course is not only a matter of your percentage. Seven artifacts are required, and you must complete every one of them **at the Developing level or better on every rubric criterion**:

| # | Required artifact |
|---|---|
| CP2 | Product Definition Brief *(the discovery brief)* |
| CP3 | Written Proposal |
| CP4 | Pitch Presentation |
| CP5 | Design Document |
| CP7 | Project Infrastructure Package |
| CP10 | MVP — Progress Demo + Repo Audit |
| CP12 | Final Pitch + Demo |

**Miss any one of them — or land at Beginning on any single criterion within one — and you do not pass the course, whatever your other grades look like.**

You have seen this shape before: in **CS 240 you had to pass the programming exam to pass the class, regardless of how your project work went.** These seven artifacts work exactly the same way.

The reason is what this course is for. You are being graded on *founding a project*, not on producing code, and strong code cannot compensate for a missing founding artifact. A prototype nobody needs, or one nobody else could pick up and continue, is not the thing you came here to build.

